{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791874394609,"sessionID":"ses_ecbf849dcffe1ywd0dEC0OOYDy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ca563657d4bf4618acf17fc6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"E:\\Work_Hub\\01_Automemory\\wt-oc-req03\\.github\\workflows\\ci.yml"},"output":"<path>E:\\Work_Hub\\01_Automemory\\wt-oc-req03\\.github\\workflows\\ci.yml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: name: arc-ci\n2: \n3: # ADR-0007 (+ macOS amendment): arc's self-tests run on ubuntu + windows (Git Bash)\n4: # + macos-latest. Red = no merge. The macOS leg exercises BSD userland (sed/stat/date\n5: # differ from GNU); bash-3.2 syntax is enforced by a static audit (no mapfile / assoc\n6: # arrays). Scanner-dependent tests skip (visibly) when a tool doesn't install, so CI is\n7: # honest and never flaky on a tool hiccup.\n8: #\n9: # CI runs ONCE per change, on the PULL REQUEST -- not again on the merge to main. The\n10: # earlier `push: branches:[main]` trigger re-ran the whole 6-job matrix on every merge, a\n11: # third pass over a tree the PR had already proven green (\"stop paying for it twice\",\n12: # d53daed). The `pull_request` trigger already covers every push to an open PR (opened +\n13: # synchronize), so a fix pushed to a red PR re-runs; a clean merge does not. This matches\n14: # the repo's own branch-then-PR rule (CLAUDE.md) -- nothing lands on main except through a\n15: # PR that CI has already gated.\n16: # NOTE (owner action, GitHub UI): turn on branch protection for `main` with \"require the\n17: # arc-ci checks to pass\" + \"require branches to be up to date before merging\". The second\n18: # option closes the one gap this leaves -- if main moves between a PR going green and its\n19: # merge, it forces the PR to re-run against the new base, so the merged tree is always a\n20: # tested tree. On a solo repo main rarely moves mid-merge, so the gap is small, but the\n21: # setting makes it zero. I cannot set branch protection from here.\n22: \n23: on:\n24:   pull_request:\n25:   workflow_dispatch:\n26: \n27: permissions:\n28:   contents: read\n29: \n30: jobs:\n31:   selftest:\n32:     strategy:\n33:       fail-fast: false\n34:       matrix:\n35:         # Three OSes on one Node, plus two Node legs on ubuntu. Cycle 2 needs the version\n36:         # dimension: node:sqlite exists only on Node 22+, and ADR-0024 makes it an OPTIONAL\n37:         # accelerator behind an equivalence gate. Both halves of that promise need a runner:\n38:         # Node 18 proves the canonical JSONL-scan path stands alone (REQ-04(b)), Node 22\n39:         # proves the accelerator is byte-identical to it. A full 3x3 matrix would be nine\n40:         # jobs to establish something two ubuntu jobs already settle.\n41:         #\n42:         # WINDOWS AND MACOS ARE SHARDED, UBUNTU IS NOT. Windows costs 1585s against ~145s on\n43:         # every ubuntu leg and 234s on macos, for the same 563 tests -- 2.82s per test against\n44:         # 0.26s, because every bats `run` spawns a process and windows process creation is\n45:         # dear. The cost is SPREAD, not concentrated (the ten heaviest files are 64% of it), so\n46:         # there is no slow file to fix and the honest response is to split.\n47:         #\n48:         # THERE IS NO WINDOWS-SIDE SAVING LEFT TO FIND. Measured 2026-07-31 (run 30611012771):\n49:         # excluding the workspace and bats' binaries from Microsoft Defender moved nothing --\n50:         # 121s vs 119s on a one-file shard and 186s vs 193s on a five-file shard, both inside\n51:         # the spread between repetitions of the same variant. The verification step explains\n52:         # why: the runner image ALREADY ships with realtime protection off and both drives\n53:         # excluded, so the lever had been pulled before we reached for it. `bats --jobs` was\n54:         # not measurable either -- it needs GNU parallel, which Git Bash does not ship and the\n55:         # download did not land.\n56:         #\n57:         # THE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER IS SET BY TWO CEILINGS, both measured, not assumed:\n58:         #\n59:         #   The heaviest single FILE. design-steel-thread.bats is 141s and one file cannot be\n60:         #   split across two runners, so from 12 shards on the heaviest shard IS that file and\n61:         #   a 13th buys nothing. Going below it means splitting the file itself, or making one\n62:         #   job run several bats processes at once (measure-windows-cost.yml asks that).\n63:         #\n64:         #   ~20 concurrent jobs on GitHub Free. Measured: a 25-job run left exactly five\n65:         #   queueing (150s, 85s, 71s, 55s, 43s). Past the cap the excess runs as a second wave\n66:         #   and the wall clock roughly doubles, so a shard count is a budget, not a free knob.\n67:         #\n68:         # 12 + 3 + 3 + ci-tier = 19 jobs, one under the cap. Windows lands at ~169s and is the\n69:         # binding leg; ubuntu is ~160s unsharded. Sharding ubuntu would cost three jobs (one per\n70:         # node version, all three run the full suite) and force windows down to 11, which comes\n71:         # out slower -- so it stays whole.\n72:         #\n73:         # MACOS GETS A THIRD SHARD FOR A REASON WORTH WRITING DOWN. It is sharded with WINDOWS\n74:         # weights, which is wrong in kind: the 2-shard split was predicted at 797/796 and ran\n75:         # 77s/161s, because the process-spawn cost that dominates windows does not dominate\n76:         # macOS. A third shard makes that error small. It does not fix it. At 238s total macOS\n77:         # is not the binding leg, so it does not earn a weights table of its own yet -- if it\n78:         # ever becomes binding, weigh it on macos-latest rather than adding shards.\n79:         #\n80:         # Minutes are free (public repo). The only currency being spent is wall clock.\n81:         #\n82:         # No test is dropped: all 563 still run on windows and on macos, just across more\n83:         # runners. `shard-tests.mjs` refuses loudly if any file would land in no shard, and the\n84:         # run step reconciles TAP lines emitted against @test lines declared -- silently\n85:         # skipping one would turn a green tick into a lie.\n86:         include:\n87:           - { os: ubuntu-latest,  node: '20', shard: 1, shards: 1 }\n88:           - { os: ubuntu-latest,  node: '18', shard: 1, shards: 1 }\n89:           - { os: ubuntu-latest,  node: '22', shard: 1, shards: 1 }\n90:           - { os: macos-latest,   node: '20', shard: 1, shards: 3 }\n91:           - { os: macos-latest,   node: '20', shard: 2, shards: 3 }\n92:           - { os: macos-latest,   node: '20', shard: 3, shards: 3 }\n93:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 1,  shards: 12 }\n94:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 2,  shards: 12 }\n95:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 3,  shards: 12 }\n96:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 4,  shards: 12 }\n97:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 5,  shards: 12 }\n98:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 6,  shards: 12 }\n99:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 7,  shards: 12 }\n100:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 8,  shards: 12 }\n101:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 9,  shards: 12 }\n102:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 10, shards: 12 }\n103:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 11, shards: 12 }\n104:           - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 12, shards: 12 }\n105:     name: selftest (${{ matrix.os }}, ${{ matrix.node }}${{ matrix.shards > 1 && format(', shard {0}/{1}', matrix.shard, matrix.shards) || '' }})\n106:     runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}\n107:     defaults:\n108:       run:\n109:         shell: bash          # Git Bash on windows-latest -- matches the target runtime\n110:     steps:\n111:       - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n112: \n113:       - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n114:         with:\n115:           node-version: ${{ matrix.node }}\n116: \n117:       - name: Install bats\n118:         run: npm install -g bats\n119: \n120:       - name: Install jq (Linux)\n121:         if: runner.os == 'Linux'\n122:         run: sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y jq\n123: \n124:       - name: Install jq (Windows)\n125:         if: runner.os == 'Windows'\n126:         run: choco install jq -y --no-progress\n127: \n128:       - name: Install jq (macOS)\n129:         if: runner.os == 'macOS'\n130:         run: command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || brew install jq\n131: \n132:       - name: Install gitleaks (best-effort)\n133:         run: |\n134:           set -u\n135:           VER=8.30.1\n136:           mkdir -p \"$HOME/bin\"\n137:           arch=x64; [ \"$(uname -m)\" = \"arm64\" ] && arch=arm64\n138:           if [ \"$RUNNER_OS\" = \"Windows\" ]; then\n139:             curl -fsSL -o gl.zip \"https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${VER}/gitleaks_${VER}_windows_x64.zip\" \\\n140:               && unzip -o gl.zip gitleaks.exe -d \"$HOME/bin\" || echo \"gitleaks install skipped\"\n141:           elif [ \"$RUNNER_OS\" = \"macOS\" ]; then\n142:             curl -fsSL -o gl.tgz \"https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${VER}/gitleaks_${VER}_darwin_${arch}.tar.gz\" \\\n143:               && tar -xzf gl.tgz -C \"$HOME/bin\" gitleaks || echo \"gitleaks install skipped\"\n144:           else\n145:             curl -fsSL -o gl.tgz \"https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${VER}/gitleaks_${VER}_linux_x64.tar.gz\" \\\n146:               && tar -xzf gl.tgz -C \"$HOME/bin\" gitleaks || echo \"gitleaks install skipped\"\n147:           fi\n148:           echo \"$HOME/bin\" >> \"$GITHUB_PATH\"\n149: \n150:       - name: Install opengrep (best-effort)\n151:         run: |\n152:           set -u\n153:           mkdir -p \"$HOME/bin\"\n154:           # PINNED, not `latest`. An unpinned scanner makes the strength of this gate whatever\n155:           # upstream shipped this morning: on 2026-08-12 a new opengrep release stopped flagging\n156:           # the planted eval() that three arc-scan tests depend on, and EVERY branch went red at\n157:           # once -- including main, which had been green hours earlier. Diagnosed by reproducing\n158:           # locally with opengrep 1.25.0 and the repo's own ruleset, which still finds it\n159:           # (1 finding, severity ERROR), so neither the rules nor the tests were at fault.\n160:           #\n161:           # The security half matters more than the red CI: an unpinned security scanner can\n162:           # silently STOP detecting a class of bug, and the only signal is a test going red. If\n163:           # those tests had been written to skip when the scanner finds nothing, the detection\n164:           # loss would have been invisible.\n165:           #\n166:           # Moving this pin is a reviewed diff that re-runs the arc-scan suite, never a bump.\n167:           base=https://github.com/opengrep/opengrep/releases/download/v1.26.0\n168:           if [ \"$RUNNER_OS\" = \"Windows\" ]; then\n169:             curl -fsSL -o \"$HOME/bin/opengrep.exe\" \"$base/opengrep_windows_x86.exe\" || echo \"opengrep install skipped\"\n170:           elif [ \"$RUNNER_OS\" = \"macOS\" ]; then\n171:             asset=opengrep_osx_x86; [ \"$(uname -m)\" = \"arm64\" ] && asset=opengrep_osx_arm64\n172:             curl -fsSL -o \"$HOME/bin/opengrep\" \"$base/$asset\" \\\n173:               && chmod +x \"$HOME/bin/opengrep\" || echo \"opengrep install skipped\"\n174:           else\n175:             curl -fsSL -o \"$HOME/bin/opengrep\" \"$base/opengrep_manylinux_x86\" \\\n176:               && chmod +x \"$HOME/bin/opengrep\" || echo \"opengrep install skipped\"\n177:           fi\n178:           echo \"$HOME/bin\" >> \"$GITHUB_PATH\"\n179: \n180:       - name: Tool banner\n181:         run: |\n182:           echo \"jq:       $(command -v jq       || echo MISSING)\"\n183:           echo \"bats:     $(command -v bats     || echo MISSING)\"\n184:           echo \"gitleaks: $(command -v gitleaks || echo MISSING)\"\n185:           echo \"opengrep: $(command -v opengrep || echo MISSING)\"\n186: \n187:       - name: Version gate\n188:         run: bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-scan/version-gate.sh\n189: \n190:       - name: Product manifests lint\n191:         # Had no CI step at all, so REQ-03's gate only ever ran against tests/fixtures/ --\n192:         # \"product-lint green\" was in the Phase-03 checkpoint contract but unenforced.\n193:         run: node .claude/scripts/core/product-lint.mjs\n194: \n195:       - name: Run bats self-tests\n196:         # Unsharded legs keep `-r tests/`. -r is kept although ADR-0021 decided tests stay\n197:         # centralised in tests/: bats does not recurse without it, and on a directory holding\n198:         # no *.bats it prints 1..0 and exits 0 -- a moved-away suite would vanish from a green\n199:         # CI without a word.\n200:         #\n201:         # Sharded legs get an explicit file list instead, which removes that particular safety\n202:         # net -- so the sharder carries its own: it packs every discovered file and exits\n203:         # non-zero if any would land in no shard, rather than quietly emitting a short list.\n204:         # `set -o pipefail` is not enough here; the list is captured first so a sharder refusal\n205:         # fails the step instead of running bats over an empty argument list.\n206:         run: |\n207:           # ---- executed-vs-declared reconciliation (em-dash incident, 2026-07-30) ----\n208:           # A U+2014 in two `@test` NAMES made bats fail to encode them into shell function\n209:           # identifiers under the C locale, so those two tests were never executed. bats\n210:           # reported it as a `# bats warning:` COMMENT among 91 `ok` lines: the step was red,\n211:           # but the reason was invisible and reads as flake. Counting the TAP lines bats\n212:           # actually emitted against the `@test` lines the files declare turns ANY\n213:           # non-execution into an annotation -- mangled name, unparseable `@test`, or a file\n214:           # bats could not load -- and it does so on every leg, not just the one that broke.\n215:           # This subsumes bats' own validator: for a `@test` whose brace is on the next line\n216:           # the plan header shrinks along with the parse, so bats sees no mismatch and this\n217:           # still does. Skips count as executed, correctly.\n218:           _tap=./.bats-tap.log\n219:           _reconcile() {\n220:             local declared=\"$1\" executed\n221:             executed=\"$(grep -cE '^(ok|not ok) ' \"$_tap\" || true)\"\n222:             echo \"reconcile: declared $declared, executed $executed\"\n223:             if [ \"$declared\" -ne \"$executed\" ]; then\n224:               echo \"::error::$(( declared - executed )) declared test(s) never executed -- a test that does not run is indistinguishable from one that passes\"\n225:               grep -nE '^# bats warning:' \"$_tap\" || true\n226:               return 1\n227:             fi\n228:             if grep -qE '^# bats warning:' \"$_tap\"; then\n229:               echo \"::error::bats emitted a warning; treating as non-execution\"\n230:               grep -nE '^# bats warning:' \"$_tap\"\n231:               return 1\n232:             fi\n233:             return 0\n234:           }\n235:           _declared() { grep -hcE '^[[:blank:]]*@test[[:blank:]]' \"$@\" | awk '{s+=$1} END{print s+0}'; }\n236: \n237:           if [ \"${{ matrix.shards }}\" -gt 1 ]; then\n238:             files=\"$(node .github/scripts/shard-tests.mjs --index ${{ matrix.shard }} --total ${{ matrix.shards }})\" || exit 1\n239:             [ -n \"$files\" ] || { echo \"::error::shard ${{ matrix.shard }}/${{ matrix.shards }} resolved to no files\"; exit 1; }\n240:             echo \"shard ${{ matrix.shard }}/${{ matrix.shards }} runs $(printf '%s\\n' \"$files\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') file(s)\"\n241:             # The file list and the elapsed seconds are printed so tests/shard-timings.json can\n242:             # be recalibrated from REAL runs instead of a throwaway measurement job. The first\n243:             # measurement was taken by timing each file in its own `bats` call, and it turned\n244:             # out to understate a grouped shard by ~2.2x while matching a single-file shard\n245:             # exactly -- so the only trustworthy source for these weights is the invocation CI\n246:             # actually performs. Grep a run for \"shard-timing:\" to harvest them.\n247:             printf 'shard-timing: files %s\\n' \"$(printf '%s ' $files)\"\n248:             _t0=$(date +%s)\n249:             set +e; bats --print-output-on-failure $files 2>&1 | tee \"$_tap\"; _rc=${PIPESTATUS[0]}; set -e\n250:             printf 'shard-timing: shard %s/%s elapsed %ss rc=%s\\n' \\\n251:               \"${{ matrix.shard }}\" \"${{ matrix.shards }}\" \"$(( $(date +%s) - _t0 ))\" \"$_rc\"\n252:             set +e; _reconcile \"$(_declared $files)\"; _rrc=$?; set -e\n253:             rm -f \"$_tap\"\n254:             [ \"$_rc\" -eq 0 ] || exit \"$_rc\"\n255:             exit \"$_rrc\"\n256:           else\n257:             set +e; bats -r --print-output-on-failure tests/ 2>&1 | tee \"$_tap\"; _rc=${PIPESTATUS[0]}; set -e\n258:             set +e; _reconcile \"$(_declared $(find tests -name '*.bats'))\"; _rrc=$?; set -e\n259:             rm -f \"$_tap\"\n260:             [ \"$_rc\" -eq 0 ] || exit \"$_rc\"\n261:             exit \"$_rrc\"\n262:           fi\n263: \n264:       - name: Test-count floor\n265:         # A suite that silently shrinks is the failure mode -r alone cannot catch. Raise this\n266:         # number when tests are added; never lower it to make a red build green.\n267:         # Counted by grep, not `bats -r --count`: measured on the windows leg that call cost\n268:         # 67s (it loads and parses every file through bats itself) for a number grep produces\n269:         # in 157ms. A gate must not cost more than what it guards.\n270:         shell: bash\n271:         run: |\n272:           n=\"$(grep -rhc '^@test ' tests/ --include='*.bats' | awk '{s+=$1} END{print s}')\"\n273:           echo \"discovered $n test(s)\"\n274:           # Raised 318 -> 891 (engine Cycle 6, Phase 00). The floor had rotted by 550: it was\n275:           # last touched when the suite held 318 tests and nothing failed as the suite tripled,\n276:           # because a floor only catches SHRINKAGE. That is the counts-rot shape of retro-log\n277:           # 2026-07-22 -- a hardcoded number nobody recomputes. Derive it, never retype it:\n278:           #   grep -rhc '^@test ' tests/ --include='*.bats' | awk '{s+=$1} END{print s}'\n279:           # Raise it to match reality; never lower it to make a red build green.\n280:           [ \"$n\" -ge 911 ] || { echo \"::error::test count $n fell below the floor of 911\"; exit 1; }\n281: \n282:   # CI tier (ADR-0006): the pinned arc-tools image runs the heavy verifiers.\n283:   # Linux-only (docker; ZAP later is linux-only too). Proves the trivy docker\n284:   # rung against the REAL image -- a genuinely vulnerable dependency must be\n285:   # caught (the native rung + the fake-docker rung are covered by bats).\n286:   ci-tier:\n287:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n288:     defaults:\n289:       run:\n290:         shell: bash\n291:     steps:\n292:       - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n293: \n294:       - name: Install jq\n295:         run: sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y jq\n296: \n297:       - name: Build the arc-tools image\n298:         run: bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-tools-image.sh build\n299: \n300:       - name: Verify trivy runs inside the image\n301:         run: bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-tools-image.sh verify\n302: \n303:       - name: Trivy docker rung catches a real vulnerable dependency\n304:         run: |\n305:           set -euo pipefail\n306:           REF=\"$(bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-tools-image.sh ref)\"\n307:           fx=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\n308:           # PyYAML 5.1 -> CVE-2020-1747 (well-known, stable in the trivy DB).\n309:           printf 'PyYAML==5.1\\n' > \"$fx/requirements.txt\"\n310:           # The image runs as non-root (uid 10001); mktemp -d is 0700, so make the\n311:           # mounted fixture world-readable or the container user can't read /src.\n312:           # (Real repo checkouts are already world-readable -- fixture-only fix.)\n313:           chmod -R a+rX \"$fx\"\n314:           out=\"$(mktemp)\"\n315:           ARC_DOCKER_IMAGE=\"$REF\" ARC_FORCE_RUNTIME=docker ROOT=\"$fx\" \\\n316:             bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-scan/adapters/trivy.sh /dev/null \"$out\"\n317:           n=\"$(jq '[.runs[].results[]?] | length' \"$out\")\"\n318:           echo \"trivy (real image) reported $n vulnerability finding(s)\"\n319:           if [ \"$n\" -eq 0 ]; then\n320:             echo \"::warning::0 findings -- mount/readability diagnostic:\"\n321:             docker run --rm -v \"$fx:/src:ro\" \"$REF\" sh -c 'id; ls -la /src; cat /src/requirements.txt || echo CANNOT-READ-SRC' || true\n322:           fi\n323:           jq -r '.runs[].results[]?.ruleId' \"$out\" | sort -u | head\n324:           [ \"$n\" -gt 0 ] || { echo \"expected >=1 finding from a vulnerable dep\"; exit 1; }\n\n(End of file - total 324 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"name: arc-ci\n\n# ADR-0007 (+ macOS amendment): arc's self-tests run on ubuntu + windows (Git Bash)\n# + macos-latest. Red = no merge. The macOS leg exercises BSD userland (sed/stat/date\n# differ from GNU); bash-3.2 syntax is enforced by a static audit (no mapfile / assoc\n# arrays). Scanner-dependent tests skip (visibly) when a tool doesn't install, so CI is\n# honest and never flaky on a tool hiccup.\n#\n# CI runs ONCE per change, on the PULL REQUEST -- not again on the merge to main. The\n# earlier `push: branches:[main]` trigger re-ran the whole 6-job matrix on every merge, a\n# third pass over a tree the PR had already proven green (\"stop paying for it twice\",\n# d53daed). The `pull_request` trigger already covers every push to an open PR (opened +\n# synchronize), so a fix pushed to a red PR re-runs; a clean merge does not. This matches\n# the repo's own branch-then-PR rule (CLAUDE.md) -- nothing lands on main except through a\n# PR that CI has already gated.\n# NOTE (owner action, GitHub UI): turn on branch protection for `main` with \"require the\n# arc-ci checks to pass\" + \"require branches to be up to date before merging\". The second\n# option closes the one gap this leaves -- if main moves between a PR going green and its\n# merge, it forces the PR to re-run against the new base, so the merged tree is always a\n# tested tree. On a solo repo main rarely moves mid-merge, so the gap is small, but the","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"E:\\Work_Hub\\01_Automemory\\wt-oc-req03\\.github\\workflows\\ci.yml","text":"name: arc-ci\n\n# ADR-0007 (+ macOS amendment): arc's self-tests run on ubuntu + windows (Git Bash)\n# + macos-latest. Red = no merge. The macOS leg exercises BSD userland (sed/stat/date\n# differ from GNU); bash-3.2 syntax is enforced by a static audit (no mapfile / assoc\n# arrays). Scanner-dependent tests skip (visibly) when a tool doesn't install, so CI is\n# honest and never flaky on a tool hiccup.\n#\n# CI runs ONCE per change, on the PULL REQUEST -- not again on the merge to main. The\n# earlier `push: branches:[main]` trigger re-ran the whole 6-job matrix on every merge, a\n# third pass over a tree the PR had already proven green (\"stop paying for it twice\",\n# d53daed). The `pull_request` trigger already covers every push to an open PR (opened +\n# synchronize), so a fix pushed to a red PR re-runs; a clean merge does not. This matches\n# the repo's own branch-then-PR rule (CLAUDE.md) -- nothing lands on main except through a\n# PR that CI has already gated.\n# NOTE (owner action, GitHub UI): turn on branch protection for `main` with \"require the\n# arc-ci checks to pass\" + \"require branches to be up to date before merging\". The second\n# option closes the one gap this leaves -- if main moves between a PR going green and its\n# merge, it forces the PR to re-run against the new base, so the merged tree is always a\n# tested tree. On a solo repo main rarely moves mid-merge, so the gap is small, but the\n# setting makes it zero. I cannot set branch protection from here.\n\non:\n  pull_request:\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  selftest:\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        # Three OSes on one Node, plus two Node legs on ubuntu. Cycle 2 needs the version\n        # dimension: node:sqlite exists only on Node 22+, and ADR-0024 makes it an OPTIONAL\n        # accelerator behind an equivalence gate. Both halves of that promise need a runner:\n        # Node 18 proves the canonical JSONL-scan path stands alone (REQ-04(b)), Node 22\n        # proves the accelerator is byte-identical to it. A full 3x3 matrix would be nine\n        # jobs to establish something two ubuntu jobs already settle.\n        #\n        # WINDOWS AND MACOS ARE SHARDED, UBUNTU IS NOT. Windows costs 1585s against ~145s on\n        # every ubuntu leg and 234s on macos, for the same 563 tests -- 2.82s per test against\n        # 0.26s, because every bats `run` spawns a process and windows process creation is\n        # dear. The cost is SPREAD, not concentrated (the ten heaviest files are 64% of it), so\n        # there is no slow file to fix and the honest response is to split.\n        #\n        # THERE IS NO WINDOWS-SIDE SAVING LEFT TO FIND. Measured 2026-07-31 (run 30611012771):\n        # excluding the workspace and bats' binaries from Microsoft Defender moved nothing --\n        # 121s vs 119s on a one-file shard and 186s vs 193s on a five-file shard, both inside\n        # the spread between repetitions of the same variant. The verification step explains\n        # why: the runner image ALREADY ships with realtime protection off and both drives\n        # excluded, so the lever had been pulled before we reached for it. `bats --jobs` was\n        # not measurable either -- it needs GNU parallel, which Git Bash does not ship and the\n        # download did not land.\n        #\n        # THE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER IS SET BY TWO CEILINGS, both measured, not assumed:\n        #\n        #   The heaviest single FILE. design-steel-thread.bats is 141s and one file cannot be\n        #   split across two runners, so from 12 shards on the heaviest shard IS that file and\n        #   a 13th buys nothing. Going below it means splitting the file itself, or making one\n        #   job run several bats processes at once (measure-windows-cost.yml asks that).\n        #\n        #   ~20 concurrent jobs on GitHub Free. Measured: a 25-job run left exactly five\n        #   queueing (150s, 85s, 71s, 55s, 43s). Past the cap the excess runs as a second wave\n        #   and the wall clock roughly doubles, so a shard count is a budget, not a free knob.\n        #\n        # 12 + 3 + 3 + ci-tier = 19 jobs, one under the cap. Windows lands at ~169s and is the\n        # binding leg; ubuntu is ~160s unsharded. Sharding ubuntu would cost three jobs (one per\n        # node version, all three run the full suite) and force windows down to 11, which comes\n        # out slower -- so it stays whole.\n        #\n        # MACOS GETS A THIRD SHARD FOR A REASON WORTH WRITING DOWN. It is sharded with WINDOWS\n        # weights, which is wrong in kind: the 2-shard split was predicted at 797/796 and ran\n        # 77s/161s, because the process-spawn cost that dominates windows does not dominate\n        # macOS. A third shard makes that error small. It does not fix it. At 238s total macOS\n        # is not the binding leg, so it does not earn a weights table of its own yet -- if it\n        # ever becomes binding, weigh it on macos-latest rather than adding shards.\n        #\n        # Minutes are free (public repo). The only currency being spent is wall clock.\n        #\n        # No test is dropped: all 563 still run on windows and on macos, just across more\n        # runners. `shard-tests.mjs` refuses loudly if any file would land in no shard, and the\n        # run step reconciles TAP lines emitted against @test lines declared -- silently\n        # skipping one would turn a green tick into a lie.\n        include:\n          - { os: ubuntu-latest,  node: '20', shard: 1, shards: 1 }\n          - { os: ubuntu-latest,  node: '18', shard: 1, shards: 1 }\n          - { os: ubuntu-latest,  node: '22', shard: 1, shards: 1 }\n          - { os: macos-latest,   node: '20', shard: 1, shards: 3 }\n          - { os: macos-latest,   node: '20', shard: 2, shards: 3 }\n          - { os: macos-latest,   node: '20', shard: 3, shards: 3 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 1,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 2,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 3,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 4,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 5,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 6,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 7,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 8,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 9,  shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 10, shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 11, shards: 12 }\n          - { os: windows-latest, node: '20', shard: 12, shards: 12 }\n    name: selftest (${{ matrix.os }}, ${{ matrix.node }}${{ matrix.shards > 1 && format(', shard {0}/{1}', matrix.shard, matrix.shards) || '' }})\n    runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}\n    defaults:\n      run:\n        shell: bash          # Git Bash on windows-latest -- matches the target runtime\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: ${{ matrix.node }}\n\n      - name: Install bats\n        run: npm install -g bats\n\n      - name: Install jq (Linux)\n        if: runner.os == 'Linux'\n        run: sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y jq\n\n      - name: Install jq (Windows)\n        if: runner.os == 'Windows'\n        run: choco install jq -y --no-progress\n\n      - name: Install jq (macOS)\n        if: runner.os == 'macOS'\n        run: command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || brew install jq\n\n      - name: Install gitleaks (best-effort)\n        run: |\n          set -u\n          VER=8.30.1\n          mkdir -p \"$HOME/bin\"\n          arch=x64; [ \"$(uname -m)\" = \"arm64\" ] && arch=arm64\n          if [ \"$RUNNER_OS\" = \"Windows\" ]; then\n            curl -fsSL -o gl.zip \"https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${VER}/gitleaks_${VER}_windows_x64.zip\" \\\n              && unzip -o gl.zip gitleaks.exe -d \"$HOME/bin\" || echo \"gitleaks install skipped\"\n          elif [ \"$RUNNER_OS\" = \"macOS\" ]; then\n            curl -fsSL -o gl.tgz \"https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${VER}/gitleaks_${VER}_darwin_${arch}.tar.gz\" \\\n              && tar -xzf gl.tgz -C \"$HOME/bin\" gitleaks || echo \"gitleaks install skipped\"\n          else\n            curl -fsSL -o gl.tgz \"https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${VER}/gitleaks_${VER}_linux_x64.tar.gz\" \\\n              && tar -xzf gl.tgz -C \"$HOME/bin\" gitleaks || echo \"gitleaks install skipped\"\n          fi\n          echo \"$HOME/bin\" >> \"$GITHUB_PATH\"\n\n      - name: Install opengrep (best-effort)\n        run: |\n          set -u\n          mkdir -p \"$HOME/bin\"\n          # PINNED, not `latest`. An unpinned scanner makes the strength of this gate whatever\n          # upstream shipped this morning: on 2026-08-12 a new opengrep release stopped flagging\n          # the planted eval() that three arc-scan tests depend on, and EVERY branch went red at\n          # once -- including main, which had been green hours earlier. Diagnosed by reproducing\n          # locally with opengrep 1.25.0 and the repo's own ruleset, which still finds it\n          # (1 finding, severity ERROR), so neither the rules nor the tests were at fault.\n          #\n          # The security half matters more than the red CI: an unpinned security scanner can\n          # silently STOP detecting a class of bug, and the only signal is a test going red. If\n          # those tests had been written to skip when the scanner finds nothing, the detection\n          # loss would have been invisible.\n          #\n          # Moving this pin is a reviewed diff that re-runs the arc-scan suite, never a bump.\n          base=https://github.com/opengrep/opengrep/releases/download/v1.26.0\n          if [ \"$RUNNER_OS\" = \"Windows\" ]; then\n            curl -fsSL -o \"$HOME/bin/opengrep.exe\" \"$base/opengrep_windows_x86.exe\" || echo \"opengrep install skipped\"\n          elif [ \"$RUNNER_OS\" = \"macOS\" ]; then\n            asset=opengrep_osx_x86; [ \"$(uname -m)\" = \"arm64\" ] && asset=opengrep_osx_arm64\n            curl -fsSL -o \"$HOME/bin/opengrep\" \"$base/$asset\" \\\n              && chmod +x \"$HOME/bin/opengrep\" || echo \"opengrep install skipped\"\n          else\n            curl -fsSL -o \"$HOME/bin/opengrep\" \"$base/opengrep_manylinux_x86\" \\\n              && chmod +x \"$HOME/bin/opengrep\" || echo \"opengrep install skipped\"\n          fi\n          echo \"$HOME/bin\" >> \"$GITHUB_PATH\"\n\n      - name: Tool banner\n        run: |\n          echo \"jq:       $(command -v jq       || echo MISSING)\"\n          echo \"bats:     $(command -v bats     || echo MISSING)\"\n          echo \"gitleaks: $(command -v gitleaks || echo MISSING)\"\n          echo \"opengrep: $(command -v opengrep || echo MISSING)\"\n\n      - name: Version gate\n        run: bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-scan/version-gate.sh\n\n      - name: Product manifests lint\n        # Had no CI step at all, so REQ-03's gate only ever ran against tests/fixtures/ --\n        # \"product-lint green\" was in the Phase-03 checkpoint contract but unenforced.\n        run: node .claude/scripts/core/product-lint.mjs\n\n      - name: Run bats self-tests\n        # Unsharded legs keep `-r tests/`. -r is kept although ADR-0021 decided tests stay\n        # centralised in tests/: bats does not recurse without it, and on a directory holding\n        # no *.bats it prints 1..0 and exits 0 -- a moved-away suite would vanish from a green\n        # CI without a word.\n        #\n        # Sharded legs get an explicit file list instead, which removes that particular safety\n        # net -- so the sharder carries its own: it packs every discovered file and exits\n        # non-zero if any would land in no shard, rather than quietly emitting a short list.\n        # `set -o pipefail` is not enough here; the list is captured first so a sharder refusal\n        # fails the step instead of running bats over an empty argument list.\n        run: |\n          # ---- executed-vs-declared reconciliation (em-dash incident, 2026-07-30) ----\n          # A U+2014 in two `@test` NAMES made bats fail to encode them into shell function\n          # identifiers under the C locale, so those two tests were never executed. bats\n          # reported it as a `# bats warning:` COMMENT among 91 `ok` lines: the step was red,\n          # but the reason was invisible and reads as flake. Counting the TAP lines bats\n          # actually emitted against the `@test` lines the files declare turns ANY\n          # non-execution into an annotation -- mangled name, unparseable `@test`, or a file\n          # bats could not load -- and it does so on every leg, not just the one that broke.\n          # This subsumes bats' own validator: for a `@test` whose brace is on the next line\n          # the plan header shrinks along with the parse, so bats sees no mismatch and this\n          # still does. Skips count as executed, correctly.\n          _tap=./.bats-tap.log\n          _reconcile() {\n            local declared=\"$1\" executed\n            executed=\"$(grep -cE '^(ok|not ok) ' \"$_tap\" || true)\"\n            echo \"reconcile: declared $declared, executed $executed\"\n            if [ \"$declared\" -ne \"$executed\" ]; then\n              echo \"::error::$(( declared - executed )) declared test(s) never executed -- a test that does not run is indistinguishable from one that passes\"\n              grep -nE '^# bats warning:' \"$_tap\" || true\n              return 1\n            fi\n            if grep -qE '^# bats warning:' \"$_tap\"; then\n              echo \"::error::bats emitted a warning; treating as non-execution\"\n              grep -nE '^# bats warning:' \"$_tap\"\n              return 1\n            fi\n            return 0\n          }\n          _declared() { grep -hcE '^[[:blank:]]*@test[[:blank:]]' \"$@\" | awk '{s+=$1} END{print s+0}'; }\n\n          if [ \"${{ matrix.shards }}\" -gt 1 ]; then\n            files=\"$(node .github/scripts/shard-tests.mjs --index ${{ matrix.shard }} --total ${{ matrix.shards }})\" || exit 1\n            [ -n \"$files\" ] || { echo \"::error::shard ${{ matrix.shard }}/${{ matrix.shards }} resolved to no files\"; exit 1; }\n            echo \"shard ${{ matrix.shard }}/${{ matrix.shards }} runs $(printf '%s\\n' \"$files\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') file(s)\"\n            # The file list and the elapsed seconds are printed so tests/shard-timings.json can\n            # be recalibrated from REAL runs instead of a throwaway measurement job. The first\n            # measurement was taken by timing each file in its own `bats` call, and it turned\n            # out to understate a grouped shard by ~2.2x while matching a single-file shard\n            # exactly -- so the only trustworthy source for these weights is the invocation CI\n            # actually performs. Grep a run for \"shard-timing:\" to harvest them.\n            printf 'shard-timing: files %s\\n' \"$(printf '%s ' $files)\"\n            _t0=$(date +%s)\n            set +e; bats --print-output-on-failure $files 2>&1 | tee \"$_tap\"; _rc=${PIPESTATUS[0]}; set -e\n            printf 'shard-timing: shard %s/%s elapsed %ss rc=%s\\n' \\\n              \"${{ matrix.shard }}\" \"${{ matrix.shards }}\" \"$(( $(date +%s) - _t0 ))\" \"$_rc\"\n            set +e; _reconcile \"$(_declared $files)\"; _rrc=$?; set -e\n            rm -f \"$_tap\"\n            [ \"$_rc\" -eq 0 ] || exit \"$_rc\"\n            exit \"$_rrc\"\n          else\n            set +e; bats -r --print-output-on-failure tests/ 2>&1 | tee \"$_tap\"; _rc=${PIPESTATUS[0]}; set -e\n            set +e; _reconcile \"$(_declared $(find tests -name '*.bats'))\"; _rrc=$?; set -e\n            rm -f \"$_tap\"\n            [ \"$_rc\" -eq 0 ] || exit \"$_rc\"\n            exit \"$_rrc\"\n          fi\n\n      - name: Test-count floor\n        # A suite that silently shrinks is the failure mode -r alone cannot catch. Raise this\n        # number when tests are added; never lower it to make a red build green.\n        # Counted by grep, not `bats -r --count`: measured on the windows leg that call cost\n        # 67s (it loads and parses every file through bats itself) for a number grep produces\n        # in 157ms. A gate must not cost more than what it guards.\n        shell: bash\n        run: |\n          n=\"$(grep -rhc '^@test ' tests/ --include='*.bats' | awk '{s+=$1} END{print s}')\"\n          echo \"discovered $n test(s)\"\n          # Raised 318 -> 891 (engine Cycle 6, Phase 00). The floor had rotted by 550: it was\n          # last touched when the suite held 318 tests and nothing failed as the suite tripled,\n          # because a floor only catches SHRINKAGE. That is the counts-rot shape of retro-log\n          # 2026-07-22 -- a hardcoded number nobody recomputes. Derive it, never retype it:\n          #   grep -rhc '^@test ' tests/ --include='*.bats' | awk '{s+=$1} END{print s}'\n          # Raise it to match reality; never lower it to make a red build green.\n          [ \"$n\" -ge 911 ] || { echo \"::error::test count $n fell below the floor of 911\"; exit 1; }\n\n  # CI tier (ADR-0006): the pinned arc-tools image runs the heavy verifiers.\n  # Linux-only (docker; ZAP later is linux-only too). Proves the trivy docker\n  # rung against the REAL image -- a genuinely vulnerable dependency must be\n  # caught (the native rung + the fake-docker rung are covered by bats).\n  ci-tier:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    defaults:\n      run:\n        shell: bash\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Install jq\n        run: sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y jq\n\n      - name: Build the arc-tools image\n        run: bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-tools-image.sh build\n\n      - name: Verify trivy runs inside the image\n        run: bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-tools-image.sh verify\n\n      - name: Trivy docker rung catches a real vulnerable dependency\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          REF=\"$(bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-tools-image.sh ref)\"\n          fx=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\n          # PyYAML 5.1 -> CVE-2020-1747 (well-known, stable in the trivy DB).\n          printf 'PyYAML==5.1\\n' > \"$fx/requirements.txt\"\n          # The image runs as non-root (uid 10001); mktemp -d is 0700, so make the\n          # mounted fixture world-readable or the container user can't read /src.\n          # (Real repo checkouts are already world-readable -- fixture-only fix.)\n          chmod -R a+rX \"$fx\"\n          out=\"$(mktemp)\"\n          ARC_DOCKER_IMAGE=\"$REF\" ARC_FORCE_RUNTIME=docker ROOT=\"$fx\" \\\n            bash .claude/scripts/review/arc-scan/adapters/trivy.sh /dev/null \"$out\"\n          n=\"$(jq '[.runs[].results[]?] | length' \"$out\")\"\n          echo \"trivy (real image) reported $n vulnerability finding(s)\"\n          if [ \"$n\" -eq 0 ]; then\n            echo \"::warning::0 findings -- mount/readability diagnostic:\"\n            docker run --rm -v \"$fx:/src:ro\" \"$REF\" sh -c 'id; ls -la /src; cat /src/requirements.txt || echo CANNOT-READ-SRC' || true\n          fi\n          jq -r '.runs[].results[]?.ruleId' \"$out\" | sort -u | head\n          [ \"$n\" -gt 0 ] || { echo \"expected >=1 finding from a vulnerable dep\"; exit 1; }","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":324,"totalLines":324,"truncated":false}},"title":".github\\workflows\\ci.yml","time":{"start":1791874394569,"end":1791874394590}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"No deploy target at root, and no open PR. Build is genuinely N/A for this repo (it's a script/harness repo, no app compiled — that's why CI doesn't run npm build). Let me check the CI workflow to confirm what \"tests green\" means and whether build is skipped.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1340966ff001Wz4rHm0Sil2Ovi","sessionID":"ses_ecbf849dcffe1ywd0dEC0OOYDy","messageID":"msg_1340950e0001PKrZwcPifGhsPH"}}
