d — I shouldn't overstep). \n\nActually the user only asked to run arc-ship. The instructions say the ship command should: lint (green), build (fail → stop). But the build failure is a genuine misconfiguration of the arc-ship command for this repo type? Or is it that this repo genuinely has no build? The prior commit d9815d70 fixed \"CI on 4efbce5f -- no npm build script\" — meaning CI does NOT expect a build. \n\nThe question: should I treat \"npm run build\" as a hard failure here and stop the ship, or treat it as N/A given this is a documentation/harness repo where CI has no build step?\n\nThe AGENTS.md under Commands lists `/arc-ship — lint → build → test → deploy in one shot`. But this repo's own CI doesn't run `npm run build`. And commit d9815d70 indicates the distribute work already handled \"no npm build script\".\n\nGiven the subagent already stopped and asked, and the user said \"Summarize the task tool output above and continue with your task\", I should continue the ship. The reasonable reading: for this repo, build = N/A (no build script, no CI build step). The ship continues: branch has upstream (origin/feat/distribute-c2-oc-session), no open PR → create PR, then `npm run test` (ci-digest wait, up to 4 attempts), then deploy: none declared → PR is the ship, emit ship.done.\n\nBut wait — should I create the PR with untracked `.opencode/` files left uncommitted? That would leave the OpenCode artifacts out of the PR. Let me check if `.opencode/` is meant to be committed. Let me look at the wiki/docs and arc-compile target opencode behavior.\n\nActually first, check git-tracked .opencode: `git log -- .opencode` returned nothing and `git ls-files .opencode` presumably empty. Let me confirm what SHOULD be there per repo conventions. Let me search docs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1340b84c4001GpkSeeCkgXI79T","sessionID":"ses_ecbf849dcffe1ywd0dEC0OOYDy","messageID":"msg_1340b1bc7001hnJ2j64U8p7lu6"}}
