files match what the compile would produce AND presumably they exist on disk.\n\nWait — but `.opencode/` is untracked (0 files tracked). The branch is `feat/distribute-c2-oc-session`. Let me check if `.opencode/` is intended to be committed in this branch's payload — is it part of the \"distribute\" scope which renders into `.opencode/`? \n\nActually the CI is dirty-diff for `.opencode/` per ADR-2001 — \"dirty-diff CI\". That means if `.opencode/` files differ from the compiled ones, CI fails. But if `.opencode/` is entirely untracked, is it checked by CI? The lint gate on this machine (`arc-compile --check --target opencode`) may only check generated-vs-source consistency, not require files to be tracked.\n\nBut wait — the distribution's point (REQ-03) is that arc's own build loop runs under OpenCode. For that to work in a fresh checkout of this repo, the `.opencode/` files must be committed. Let me check the git history on main for `.opencode/`... `git ls-files .opencode` = 0, so nothing tracked. Let me check whether `.gitignore` ignores it or whether the plan intends `.opencode/` to be committed.\n\nThe plan (PLAN-distribute.md line 318) says OUTPUTS: `.codex/ · .opencode/ · skills-only bundle`. And line 199 mentions \"Adding `.opencode/` beside two undeclared directories would be...\" — that's about the source dirs being undeclared, ADR-2004 was resolved, so `.opencode/` became a declared output.\n\nThe key question for shipping: does the branch's diff include `.opencode/`? `git ls-files` says no tracked files. So the distribute payload (`.opencode/` opencode output) was never committed. But the whole point of this branch `feat/distribute-c2-oc-session` is \"REQ-03: arc's own commit→review→ship loop runs inside OpenCode\". \n\nHmm, but maybe `.opencode/` is intentionally untracked because it's a local install target in this repo (like how the repo itself is installed via sync-to-project). Let me check `.gitignore` for `.opencode`.\n\nActually `find` counted 3753