"build\" is its self-tests (bats product-lint etc.) which run on the PR. The ship command's step 3 handles the PR-based flow with `npm run test` being the CI read.\n- The previous fix commit d9815d70 explicitly says \"no npm build script\" — meaning the distribute cycle already KNOWS this repo has no build step and handles shipping accordingly.\n\nSo the correct continuation: treat build as N/A (skip), proceed to the PR path: create PR, then `npm run test` (ci-digest --wait, which gates on CI), no deploy target → PR is the ship, emit spine event.\n\nBut wait — should I check whether `package.json` change is needed? The last commit df42bf2a \"chore(distribute): merge origin/main into the cycle branch; sync golden regenerated\" — and the package.json was NOT modified (no build added). Actually let me check if the distribute lane intentionally handles this. Let me look at whether there's an expectation in the lane docs that arc-ship skips build. \n\nActually, let me reconsider: The user's ask was to summarize the subagent output and continue. The subagent's recommendation offered two options: (a) add a build script if one is genuinely expected, or (b) clarify the intended ship step. \n\nLet me verify: does CI expect a build? No — CI runs bats + product-lint + version-gate. No npm build. So \"build\" is genuinely N/A by design. The right continuation for /arc-ship on this repo is: build is a no-op (N/A), proceed to PR creation + test watching.\n\nBut also — the `.opencode/` untracked dir. Let me check if the branch needs it committed for REQ-03 to be verified. Let me look at the distribute runbook / the branch's recent work to see if there's a pending commit checklist. Actually, more importantly: let me check what the PR diff would contain. Let me check git status of untracked files that would matter.\n\nActually, I should be careful not to overstep. The /arc-ship command instructs: run steps in order, STOP at first failure. It already failed at step 2. But the user explicitly told me to \"continue with your task.\" \n\nI think the pragmatic resolution: since `npm run build` doesn't exist in this repo and CI has no build step (this is by design per commit d9815d70), treat build as not-applicable and continue the ship through the PR path. But the `.opencode/` files being untracked means the PR wouldn't include them — but they're generated outputs, and per ADR-2001 dirty-diff CI, if they aren't committed nothing fails (dirty-diff checks tracked files against compiled output). Committing generated `.opencode/` for the OpenCode target may be the INTENT of the distribute lane though.\n\nHmm — let me check whether this branch is supposed to include `.opencode/`. Let me look at the distribute lane PROGRESS to see where the OC session work stands. This is the \"wt-oc-req03\" workspace — the \"OC session REQ-03\" test. The branch name is literally `feat/distribute-c2-oc-session`. The Cycle 2 kickoff commit mentions \"REQ-03 under OpenCode\".\n\nREQ-03: \"arc's own commit → review → ship loop runs inside OpenCode on a non-Anthropic model-policy profile and the same three negative fixtures are refused\".\n\nSo the deliverable of the session may just be a transcript/evidence. The `.opencode/` generated files — are they needed? For OpenCode to run arc's commands inside THIS repo, the `.opencode/` commands must exist in the repo. So they likely SHOULD be committed. But maybe the branch plan is to leave them untracked in this repo and only verify via the OC session.\n\nI think I should not silently commit a large generated tree. The safest continuation: this is genuinely ambiguous. But the user said continue. Let me check the distribute PROGRESS.md to see the session status and whether `.opencode/` commit is expected there.\n\nLet me read the distribute lane PROGRESS and the runbook quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1340c28b8001OBFTevzBykHC1P","sessionID":"ses_ecbf849dcffe1ywd0dEC0OOYDy","messageID":"msg_1340b9371001NQMqOBxvEM